---
description: Prefer polymorphism over kind checks when NPC and user actors behave differently
alwaysApply: true
---

# NPC vs user: use polymorphism, not `kind` branches

When NPC and human actors need different behavior, express the difference by
**overriding in a subclass** (or a kind-keyed factory/map), not by branching on
`p.kind === 'npc'` at each call site.

Reference implementation: `NpcCharacter3D` in [Character3D.ts](src/character/Character3D.ts)
overrides `nameplateColor`; `CampusScene` picks the class via `peerAvatarByKind`.

```ts
// BAD — color decided at every call site
avatar = new Character3D(look, p.kind === 'npc' ? '#f87171' : '#fff')
avatar.setNameplate(name, voiceOn, p.kind === 'npc' ? '#f87171' : '#fff')

// GOOD — behavior owned by the class
class Character3D {
  protected get nameplateColor(): string { return '#fff' }
}
class NpcCharacter3D extends Character3D {
  protected override get nameplateColor(): string { return '#f87171' }
}

const peerAvatarByKind = {
  user: (look: CharacterLook) => new Character3D(look),
  npc: (look: CharacterLook) => new NpcCharacter3D(look),
} as const

avatar = peerAvatarByKind[p.kind](look)
```

Applies to new NPC/user differences going forward (visuals, interaction,
capabilities). Existing `isUserPresence` / `isNpcPresence` filters for
roster, DM, WebRTC, capacity, and minigames stay as they are — do not
refactor them unless explicitly asked.
